Print the call stack when an exception goes uncaught, which is what macOS does here.

An uncaught ObjC exception is how a guest application dies most often, and the default handler
printed the class and the reason and nothing else. That names WHAT failed and never WHO called
it, so chasing one to its caller has meant adding a temporary probe every single time.

The frames are already captured: corefoundation NSException fills in callStackSymbols when the
exception is raised. This only prints what was collected anyway, and only on the path where the
process is about to die, so it costs nothing anywhere else.

It paid for itself immediately. CMake.app, a Qt application, dies intermittently on

    -[__NSCFString colorUsingColorSpaceName:device:]: unrecognized selector

and one run with this in place gave the whole route:

    QApplicationPrivate::initializeWidgetPalettesFromTheme
      qt_mac_toQColor(NSColor const*)
        -[NSColor_catalog colorUsingColorSpaceName:device:]
          the receiver of the inner call is not a colour

The receiver class DIFFERS BETWEEN RUNS, O2Color once and __NSCFString the next, which is what
freed memory looks like rather than a wrong type. See task #227.

--- a/src/NSException.m
+++ b/src/NSException.m
@@ -33,6 +33,22 @@
 static void printExceptionInformation(id exception)
 {
     NSLog(@"Terminating app due to uncaught exception '%@', reason: '%@'", NSStringFromClass([exception class]), [exception reason]);
+
+    /*
+     * AND WHERE IT CAME FROM, which macOS prints here and this did not.
+     *
+     * An uncaught exception is how a guest application dies most often, and the two lines above
+     * name WHAT failed and never WHO called it. Chasing one to its caller has meant adding a
+     * temporary probe every time. The frames are already on the exception, captured by
+     * corefoundation NSException callStackSymbols; this only prints what was collected anyway.
+     */
+    NSArray *frames = nil;
+    if ([exception respondsToSelector: @selector(callStackSymbols)])
+        frames = [exception callStackSymbols];
+
+    NSUInteger count = [frames count];
+    for (NSUInteger i = 0; i < count; i++)
+        NSLog(@"  %lu %@", (unsigned long) i, [frames objectAtIndex: i]);
 }
 
 __attribute__((constructor))
